{
  "case_id": "2020-19-863",
  "document_hash": "dd9c73c93a20ee79412ce7b1086ff968685e3e4a30812095f8302ee4e2dd7ea7",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "David Zimmer",
      "case_id": "2020-19-863",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dd9c73c93a20ee79412ce7b1086ff968685e3e4a30812095f8302ee4e2dd7ea7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dd9c73c93a20-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The statute's text and the changes\nCongress made in IIRIRA unambiguously establish\nthat a notice to appear is a specific notice\ndocument. As a textual matter, the government\nsimply cannot explain why Congress used the\nphrase \"a notice\" if what it really meant was\nsimply notice in the abstract. Even more\nremarkably, though, the government all but\nconcedes that accepting its interpretation means\nthat Congress made significant changes to the\nstatute in IIRIRA for no reason at all."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice KAVANAUGH",
      "case_id": "2020-19-863",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dd9c73c93a20ee79412ce7b1086ff968685e3e4a30812095f8302ee4e2dd7ea7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dd9c73c93a20-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The statute requires written notice, and, as I understand it, your client did receive written notice of everything in Section 1229(a). So why doesn't that end the case?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. YANG (Respondent)",
      "case_id": "2020-19-863",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dd9c73c93a20ee79412ce7b1086ff968685e3e4a30812095f8302ee4e2dd7ea7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dd9c73c93a20-0001-0009",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Board of Immigration Appeals adopted the best reading of the INA in concluding that Section 1229(a)'s written notice requirement permits written notice to be provided in two documents: an NTA form and a notice of hearing. That conclusion flows directly from the statutory text."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2020-19-863",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dd9c73c93a20ee79412ce7b1086ff968685e3e4a30812095f8302ee4e2dd7ea7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dd9c73c93a20-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "EOIR issues hearing notices as the adjudicator of the charges, and serving an alien with an NTA form containing those charges has traditionally been viewed as a prosecutorial function, not one performed by the neutral adjudicator. For DHS, once EOIR issues a hearing notice, it would be administratively difficult to act with sufficient speed to combine the NTA form with that notice and re-serve both on the alien."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. YANG",
      "case_id": "2020-19-863",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dd9c73c93a20ee79412ce7b1086ff968685e3e4a30812095f8302ee4e2dd7ea7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dd9c73c93a20-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 64,
        "start": 64
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. YANG: Well, actually, I think that's a fairly helpful hypothetical for us because this Court has already addressed notices to appeal, and when they omit the signature requirement that was required to be on it, the Court determined that that's okay. You can do that after the fact and that the essential question is whether notice is adequately conveyed."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2020-19-863",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dd9c73c93a20ee79412ce7b1086ff968685e3e4a30812095f8302ee4e2dd7ea7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dd9c73c93a20-0003-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 68,
        "start": 68
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Justice KAVANAUGH: Good morning, Mr. Yang. I just want to make sure I understand the ramifications here of each side's position. If you were to lose, the IJ, the immigration judge, could still reject cancellation of removal and remove the non-citizen; it would just be discretionary rather than mandatory. Is that correct?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Barrett",
      "case_id": "2020-19-863",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dd9c73c93a20ee79412ce7b1086ff968685e3e4a30812095f8302ee4e2dd7ea7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dd9c73c93a20-0003-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 73,
        "start": 72
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BARRETT: No, no, no. I'm not saying you rely on the immigration court setting a date. I'm saying that once a non-citizen is put in a detention facility, can't DHS at that point -- you know, Justice Alito talked about issuing notices to appear that maybe have estimated dates. I mean, couldn't you do that at that point?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2020-19-863",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dd9c73c93a20ee79412ce7b1086ff968685e3e4a30812095f8302ee4e2dd7ea7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dd9c73c93a20-0003-0004",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 76,
        "start": 76
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Congress specified in 1229(a) that written notice should be required, and Congress specified both the form and the substance of the notice. The form is that it has to be in writing and it has to be served either personally or by mail."
    },
